## What I did

As flagged last round, I extended the audit to a **new buildable module, `core`** (355 source files, 132 tests). First I confirmed it compiles and tests pass here via `-am` (also-make), establishing a verifiable sandbox — then applied the same **`Locale.ROOT` hardening** theme.

All 8 locale-dependent case conversions in `core/src/main` fixed across 7 files. Each is a machine-readable identifier where the default-locale default is a latent bug (Turkish/Azeri "I"):
- **`JWTClientCredentialsProvider`** — `toUpperCase()` feeding `Enum.valueOf(KeystoreFormat.class, ...)`; the clearest bug (Turkish locale would make `valueOf` throw on config input).
- **`SdJwt` / `IssuerSignedJWT` / `SdJwtVP`** — hash-algorithm names written into **signed SD-JWT claims**; locale-independence matters for protocol correctness (a comment there even notes "Normalize to lowercase to comply with IANA registered hash algorithm names").
- **`BasicAuthHelper`** — HTTP auth-scheme comparison (test-covered).
- **`MultivaluedHashMapValueSerializer`** — bean property-name derivation.
- **`VCFormat`** — credential-format detection from scope; here I also **removed a duplicate `toLowerCase()` call** (it was invoked twice on the same string) by computing it once.

I classified all 8 as machine identifiers, not display text, so `Locale.ROOT` is unambiguously correct — consistent with the display-text judgment call I made for `ObjectUtil.capitalize` in `common`.

